    <div id="empire-stats-panel" data-empire="Elamite Confederation"></div>


    <div class="profile-section-block">
        <p class="profile-section-title">Foundation</p>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#enmebaragesi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/enmebaragesi.jpg" alt="Enmebaragesi" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#enmebaragesi">Enmebaragesi</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mebaragsi,_King_of_Kish_(transcription_of_fragment,_original_in_Iraq_National_Museum).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">पाटलिपुत्र, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Elamite civilisation emerged from the highland-lowland geography of southwestern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iran">Iran</a>, a structural duality that defined its political character across two millennia. The lowland capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#susa">Susa</a>, heavily influenced by Mesopotamian culture, coexisted with the highland core centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#anshan">Anshan</a>, and this bifurcation meant that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#elam">Elam</a> could lose its lowlands to conquest while retaining an unconquered interior from which revival was always possible. Early recorded history places Elam as a target of Sumerian raids, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sumerian-king-list">Sumerian King List</a> crediting King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#enmebaragesi">Enmebaragesi</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kish">Kish</a> with a victory over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#elamites">Elamites</a>, traditionally dated to around 2700 BCE. The first genuine unification came under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kutik-inshushinak">Kutik-Inshushinak</a> (Puzur-Inshushinak) of Awan around 2100 BCE, who exploited the collapse of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire</a> to free Susa, bring Anshan and the surrounding highlands under his rule, and campaign deep into northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>. He promoted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#linear-elamite">Linear Elamite</a> script alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cuneiform">cuneiform</a> as an assertion of cultural independence. Though his state collapsed after his death and Elam fell under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#third-dynasty-of-ur">Third Dynasty of Ur</a>, this founding moment established the template for all subsequent Elamite revivals: exploit a Mesopotamian power vacuum, unite highland and lowland, and assert a distinct cultural identity.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block">
        <p class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</p>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#chogha-zanbil"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chogha-zanbil.jpg" alt="Chogha Zanbil" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#chogha-zanbil">Chogha Zanbil</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D8%B2%DB%8C%DA%AF%D9%88%D8%B1%D8%A7%D8%AA_%DA%86%D8%BA%D8%A7_%D8%B2%D9%86%D8%A8%DB%8C%D9%84.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Mobina ghanbaryan, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Elamite economy was structured around the complementary resources of its dual geography, with highland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#anshan">Anshan</a> supplying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#timber">timber</a>, metals, stone, and livestock while lowland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#susa">Susa</a> controlled agricultural surplus and access to Mesopotamian trade networks. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tin">Tin</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#silver">silver</a>, and other highland goods moved westward into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> in exchange for grain, textiles, and luxury goods, establishing commercial relationships that persisted even during periods of political conflict. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sukkalmah-period">Sukkalmah period</a>, roughly 1970 to 1500 BCE, produced the most institutionally sophisticated economic administration in Elamite history, with Susa functioning as a genuine commercial capital whose contracts, loans, and land transactions survive in large numbers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cuneiform">cuneiform</a> tablets. Metalworking, especially <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bronze-working">bronze-working</a>, represented a consistent Elamite strength, attested by finds such as the massive bronze statue of Queen Napir-Asu. The construction of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#chogha-zanbil">Chogha Zanbil</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ziggurat">ziggurat</a> complex under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#untash-napirisha">Untash-Napirisha</a> in the thirteenth century BCE, a structure some 105 metres square at its base and now a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>, demonstrates the mobilisation of enormous surplus resources and a deliberate policy of religious inclusivity, with temples to highland and lowland deities incorporated into a single monumental complex intended to unify Elamite political identity around a shared sacred centre.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block">
        <p class="profile-section-title">Military</p>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#kindattu"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kindattu.jpg" alt="Kindattu" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#kindattu">Kindattu</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lament_for_ur_(cropped).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Dfg13, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Elamite military power operated cyclically, surging whenever Mesopotamian rivals weakened and receding when those rivals consolidated. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#elamite-sack-of-ur">sack of Ur</a> around 2004 BCE, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#shimashki-dynasty">Shimashki</a> king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kindattu">Kindattu</a> captured Ibbi-Sin, the last ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#third-dynasty-of-ur">Third Dynasty of Ur</a>, and ended that dynasty's domination of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#elam">Elam</a>, represents the first major offensive achievement. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sukkalmah-period">Sukkalmah period</a> produced Elam's most remarkable diplomatic-military moment: around 1765 BCE, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sukkalmah">sukkalmah</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#siwe-palar-khuppak">Siwe-Palar-Khuppak</a> led a coalition that conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#eshnunna">Eshnunna</a> and was addressed as father, denoting superior rank, by rulers of the stature of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hammurabi">Hammurabi</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#babylon">Babylon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#zimri-lim">Zimri-Lim</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mari">Mari</a>. The military zenith came under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#shutrukid-dynasty">Shutrukid dynasty</a>: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#shutruk-nahhunte">Shutruk-Nahhunte</a> and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kutir-nahhunte">Kutir-Nahhunte</a> overthrew the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kassite-dynasty">Kassite dynasty</a> of Babylon around 1158 to 1155 BCE, carrying off the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#victory-stele-of-naram-sin">Victory Stele of Naram-Sin</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#code-of-hammurabi">Code of Hammurabi</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#susa">Susa</a> as war trophies. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#shilhak-inshushinak">Shilhak-Inshushinak</a>, Elamite territory extended from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tigris">Tigris</a> deep into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iranian-plateau">Iranian plateau</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#neo-elamite-period">Neo-Elamite period</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#humban-nikash-i">Humban-nikash I</a> fought the Assyrian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sargon-ii">Sargon II</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-der">Battle of Der</a> in 720 BCE, a victory according to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#babylonian-chronicles">Babylonian Chronicle</a>, demonstrating that Elamite military capability could still challenge the greatest power of the era.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block">
        <p class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</p>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#hammurabi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hammurabi.jpg" alt="Hammurabi" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#hammurabi">Hammurabi</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:F0182_Louvre_Code_Hammourabi_Bas-relief_Sb8_rwk.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Mbzt, CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sukkalmah-period">Sukkalmah system</a>, which took shape under the Epartid rulers, the dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ebarat-ii">Ebarat II</a>, in the early second millennium BCE, represents the most sophisticated constitutional innovation in Elamite history. It created a tripartite governing hierarchy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sukkalmah">sukkalmah</a> as grand regent, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sukkal">sukkal</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#elam">Elam</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#shimashki-dynasty">Shimashki</a> as designated heir, and the sukkal of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#susa">Susa</a>, with succession commonly passing laterally between brothers before descending to the next generation. This arrangement provided a stability unusual in ancient Near Eastern governance, allowing the confederation to function coherently across multiple generations with comparatively few of the succession crises that destabilised contemporaneous Mesopotamian states. Elamite law drew on both indigenous traditions and Mesopotamian legal frameworks, though no comprehensive Elamite legal code comparable to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hammurabi">Hammurabi</a>'s survives. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kidinuid-dynasty">Kidinuid dynasty</a>'s revival around 1500 BCE introduced the unifying title <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#king-of-anshan-and-susa">King of Anshan and Susa</a>, reasserting Elamite identity over the Mesopotamian influences that had dominated Susa during periods of subjugation. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#neo-elamite-period">Neo-Elamite period</a> demonstrated the system's terminal vulnerability: between 699 and 646 BCE, approximately twelve kings and rival claimants occupied the throne in fifty-three years, as Assyrian pressure and cycles of assassination destroyed the institutional coherence that had sustained earlier peaks.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block">
        <p class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</p>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#code-of-hammurabi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/code-of-hammurabi.jpg" alt="Code of Hammurabi" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#code-of-hammurabi">Code of Hammurabi</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:P1050763_Louvre_code_Hammurabi_face_rwk.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Mbzt, CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#elam">Elam</a>'s most consequential regional impact was its persistent disruption of Mesopotamian hegemony at critical junctures. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#elamite-sack-of-ur">sack of Ur</a> permanently reshaped Mesopotamian political geography. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#elamite-conquest-of-babylon-c-1158-bce">defeat of the Kassite dynasty</a> in the mid-twelfth century BCE ended more than four centuries of Kassite rule and created the conditions for the Babylonian revival under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nebuchadnezzar-i">Nebuchadnezzar I</a>, who later recovered the statue of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#marduk">Marduk</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#susa">Susa</a>. The removal of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#code-of-hammurabi">Code of Hammurabi</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#victory-stele-of-naram-sin">Victory Stele of Naram-Sin</a> to Susa, while acts of conquest, inadvertently preserved these monuments; their recovery by French excavators at Susa around 1900 fundamentally shaped modern understanding of ancient Mesopotamian law and kingship. Most significantly, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#elamite-language">Elamite language</a> and bureaucratic practice were absorbed into the Achaemenid Persian state, appearing alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#old-persian">Old Persian</a> and Babylonian on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#behistun-inscription">Behistun Inscription</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#darius-i">Darius I</a> and serving as the primary language of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#persepolis-fortification-tablets">Persepolis Fortification Tablets</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block">
        <p class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</p>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#sennacherib"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sennacherib.jpg" alt="Sennacherib" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#sennacherib">Sennacherib</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sanherib-tr-4271.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Timo Roller, CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Neo-Elamite terminal decline unfolded in two phases. Internal dynastic collapse, with around twelve rulers in fifty-three years between 699 and 646 BCE, reflected a system incapable of stable succession under sustained Assyrian pressure. Provocations including the handover and death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sennacherib">Sennacherib</a>'s son Ashur-nadin-shumi in Elamite captivity in 694 BCE triggered devastating reprisals. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#assyrian-destruction-of-susa">Ashurbanipal's destruction of Susa</a> around 646 BCE demolished its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ziggurat">ziggurat</a>, looted its treasuries, desecrated royal tombs, and carried the bones of Elamite kings to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#assyrian-empire">Assyria</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#humban-haltash-iii">Humban-haltash III</a>, the last significant king of the period, was later captured and paraded as a prisoner at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nineveh">Nineveh</a>. Fragmented rump states survived the subsequent Assyrian collapse, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#anshan">Anshan</a> had already passed to the Persian dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#teispes">Teispes</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a>'s absorption of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#elam">Elam</a> into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> in the 540s and 530s BCE ended political independence, yet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#susa">Susa</a> was soon elevated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#darius-i">Darius I</a> into a principal Persian royal capital.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary"><em>The Elamite Confederation's enduring significance lies not in imperial domination but in institutional and cultural survival: a civilisation that outlasted conquest by becoming indispensable to its conquerors.</em></p>

